1. In series: every dependency must be up

  your service                         99.95%       22 min/month
  your database                        99.95%       22 min/month
  the embedding endpoint               99.90%       43 min/month
  the model endpoint                   99.90%       43 min/month
  the request, failures independent    99.70%      129 min/month
  the request, provider fails as one   99.80%       86 min/month

2. A fallback provider: only as independent as their failures are

  share of outages in common    available         downtime
  0%                             99.9999%      0 min/month
  10%                            99.9899%      4 min/month
  50%                            99.9500%     22 min/month

3. Latency in series: the p95 of a sum is not the sum of the p95s

  embed the question     p50  0.25s   p95  0.48s
  retrieve               p50  0.05s   p95  0.08s
  model call 1           p50  1.60s   p95  3.63s
  model call 2           p50  1.60s   p95  3.63s
  model call 3           p50  1.60s   p95  3.65s
  sum of the p95s                    11.47s
  p95 of the request                  8.85s

  Budgeting each step at its own p95 over-states the request's p95 by 30%: the
  slow steps rarely coincide. A budget is set on the whole, and measured there.

4. Fan-out: a request that waits for every one of n parallel calls

  each call slow 1% of the time
    1 call    request slow  1.0%   (simulated 1.0%)
    5 calls   request slow  4.9%   (simulated 5.0%)
   10 calls   request slow  9.6%   (simulated 9.7%)
   20 calls   request slow 18.2%   (simulated 18.4%)
   50 calls   request slow 39.5%   (simulated 39.3%)

5. Retries: fewer failures on a good day, more load on a bad one

  call failure rate     fails, 2 retries  calls per request
  2%                             0.0008%               1.02
  20%                            0.8000%               1.24
  100%                         100.0000%               3.00

  At a 2% transient rate retries are nearly free. When the provider is down
  they triple the traffic aimed at it, which is Chapter 26's breaker's job.
